</style>
<div data-framer-root
    class="framer-8rx25 framer-xuxaT framer-qGQ1a framer-qbhXX framer-7pd5f framer-g1qRI framer-HhrXh framer-2awtn framer-cYAhq framer-1avdukk"
    style="min-height:100vh;width:auto;display:contents">
    <article class="framer-1lmri52" data-framer-name="Article">
        <div class="framer-1hkkcxw" data-framer-name="Article Header">
            <div class="framer-kan86p" data-framer-name="Metadata">
                <div class="framer-7e0rtl" data-framer-name="Category Chip">
                    <div class="framer-178ohia" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="transform:none">
                        <p dir="auto" style="--framer-font-size:12px;--framer-text-color:rgb(17, 17, 17)"
                            class="framer-text">Method</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="framer-17xt5g6" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="transform:none">
                    <p dir="auto" style="--framer-font-size:14px;--framer-line-height:1.4em;--framer-text-color:#777777"
                        class="framer-text"><time datetime="2026-09-22T00:00:00.000Z">September 22, 2026</time></p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="framer-atjf4x" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="transform:none">
                <h1 dir="auto"
                    style="--framer-font-size:clamp(32px, 6vw, 56px);--framer-letter-spacing:-0.04em;--framer-line-height:1.05em;--framer-text-color:#111111"
                    class="framer-text">While Not Good Yet</h1>
            </div>
            <div class="framer-f9oopq" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="transform:none">
                <p dir="auto"
                    style="--framer-letter-spacing:-0.01em;--framer-line-height:1.55em;--framer-text-color:rgb(102, 102, 102)"
                    class="framer-text">The Ralph loop extends the while loop to questions no machine can settle: a
                    readable chart, a fluent text, an honest forecast. It only works under four conditions and one of
                    them can be counted.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <a class="drz-logo-hero" href="https://sprezzature.ai/ralph-eyeball-loop.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <img src="<?= ASSETS ?>/images/blog/ralph-eyeball.webp"
                alt="A stitched leather eyeball, drawn like an antique engraving: the eye that looks on every turn of the loop."
                loading="lazy">
            <span class="drz-hero-cta">The Ralph Eyeball Loop, at sprezzature.ai&nbsp;↗</span>
        </a>
        <div class="framer-gln7zt" data-framer-name="Content" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer"
            style="transform:none">
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">"While the counter is below ten, go round
                again." It is one of the oldest sentences in computing. It works for a reason nobody notices any more:
                the machine knows how to answer the question it is being asked. Comparing a counter to ten costs one
                clock cycle, a single one of the elementary operations a processor runs through billions of times per
                second. And the answer is exact. The whole power of the loop sits in that cheap stopping condition.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The questions that decide the quality of a piece of work are not so obliging, far from it. Is this chart readable? Does this paragraph follow from
                the one before? Does this forecasting model deserve to go to production? None of them settles in a clock
                cycle, let alone for free. So we pulled them out of our loops and pushed them to the end, into a single review, done by
                hand, once, by someone who has already worked a lot.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The Ralph loop puts them back. It keeps the
                skeleton of the while loop, save that a judgement now sits where the test used to be: a human eye, a vision model
                (an artificial-intelligence program that describes what it sees in an image), a dataset held out on the
                side. On the face of it, the move looks modest. It changes what can be automated. It also opens a precise trap, and one that takes a number rather than an adjective.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Two materials put the loop to the test, figures and prose. The second
                holds a surprise: the instrument used there was invented by Mark Twain to mock a translation of his
                own story, before computer vision borrowed it from him a century later. It comes home here. As for the
                trap, it has a mechanism of its own: consult the same judge often enough and the loop learns the
                judge's quirks instead of the subject, which statisticians call overfitting. It takes a number, it
                shows up on real data and it yields two rules of conduct. An autonomous loop is worth less for the talent
                of the actor than for the quality of the judge it is given and for being forbidden to corrupt that
                judge.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>The loop in one line
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The name comes from the Australian engineer
                <a class="framer-text" href="https://ghuntley.com/ralph/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Geoffrey
                    Huntley</a>, who described in July 2025 what he calls the Ralph technique<sup class="drz-ref"><a
                        class="framer-text" href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>, after Ralph Wiggum,
                the Simpsons schoolboy whose answers miss the point with admirable constancy. It fits in one line of
                shell, the program you type orders into in a terminal:
            </p>
            <code class="drz-code">while :; do cat PROMPT.md | claude-code ; done</code>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Word for word: while true, feed the contents
                of the file <code class="framer-text">PROMPT.md</code> to the agent, then start over. You write down in
                a file what you want; you feed it in a loop to a coding agent, that is, a language model (the program
                behind conversational assistants) allowed here to read and write files and to run commands; you never
                let it leave. The surprise is that this produces usable software rather than a heap of incoherent files.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Why does it work? A language model works
                inside a context window, the amount of text it keeps in view during an exchange. That window is short
                and degrades as it fills: late in a long conversation, the model loses track of its own decisions. The
                code repository, the project folder with the history of its versions, does not degrade. On every turn of
                Huntley's loop, the agent starts from scratch, rereads the real state of the files, does one thing,
                writes it down. Progress accumulates on disk, never in the model's memory. The forgetting is the
                feature.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Huntley does not sell his technique as a
                solution: he writes that it is "deterministically bad in a non-deterministic world," that it only suits
                a brand-new project, that it demands an experienced engineer at the helm and that its output comes out
                "undercooked, cooked or cooked with unspecified latent behaviours." He reports a contract billed
                50,000 dollars and delivered for 297 dollars of compute; that figure is his and nothing in this
                article verifies it.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">One detail of his command line deserves a
                pause, because it is this article's whole subject. <code class="framer-text">while :</code> means
                "while true": the condition is empty. The original loop has no test at all; it runs until a human stops
                it. Everything interesting begins when you put something back into that condition. Huntley himself
                extended the idea in January 2026, arguing the pattern holds for every task<sup class="drz-ref"><a
                        class="framer-text" href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>, without giving its
                skeleton. That is what the rest of this article tries to do.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>The cycle: act, render, judge, decide
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A four-step cycle is enough to describe every
                variant you will meet. Act: produce one change, a single one, so that you later know what to credit for
                the result. Render: turn that change into an object you can put in front of you and examine, an image, a
                printed text, a table of measurements; that object is called an artifact. Judge: examine the artifact.
                Decide: keep or discard, writing down the reason.</p>
            <div class="drz-art-fig drz-art-fig--wide"><img src="<?= ASSETS ?>/images/blog/ralph-loop-en.svg"
                    alt="A four-step cycle: act, render, judge, decide, then back to act while budget remains, with an exit when the result satisfies or the budget is spent."
                    loading="lazy">
                <p class="framer-text drz-art-figcap">The shared skeleton. The loops described below differ only
                    in what gets rendered and in who does the judging.</p>
            </div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The step most often skipped is the second, which is also the one that looks most
                dispensable.
                Without a render, the judgement bears on an intention rather than a result; the loop spins without
                learning anything. The fourth is the one you regret not doing: without a written reason, you will not
                know, three weeks later, how many ideas were tried before the one that survived. That count of attempts is not paperwork: it is a statistical quantity on which the validity of
                everything else depends.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The idea is not new, which is rather
                reassuring: it goes by a different name in every trade. Software engineering has known it for thirty
                years as the oracle problem, the difficulty of telling correct behaviour from faulty behaviour when no
                specification does it for you; a 2015 survey concludes that the last source of truth remains the
                human<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Machine learning
                rediscovered it through failure: a language model left to its own
                resources corrects itself poorly and sometimes degrades in the attempt<sup class="drz-ref"><a
                        class="framer-text" href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. A July 2026 survey of
                1,250 papers sums everything up in one sentence: every improvement loop is a bet that some signal can
                stand in for human judgement<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The sturdier the signal, the better the loop, which moves the question from the agent to the judge it is given.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>First material: figures
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">You would expect, in 2026, any model to
                produce a professional figure unaided. In reality, figures remain among the hardest objects to
                delegate: an image that came out of a machine is recognizable at first glance. That gap between the
                promise and the output makes figures the loop's natural first material.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A sentence is checked by reading it again.
                A chart is not. The code that draws a figure can be entirely correct and the figure wrong all
                the same: a label sliding out of frame, a legend sitting on top of the data, two points exactly
                superimposed, a red-and-green palette collapsing into one muddy hue for anyone who cannot tell them
                apart. None of that shows up when you read the source, only when you look at the rendered result.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">That is the discipline the
                <a class="framer-text" href="https://sprezzature.ai/ralph-eyeball-loop.html" target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener">Ralph Eyeball Loop</a><sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-6">6</a></sup> formalizes, at <a class="framer-text"
                    href="https://sprezzature.ai/figures.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sprezzature.ai</a>:
                render, look, fix the source, repeat, until there is nothing left to spot. The tool converts the source
                into an image with a deterministic engine, no model and no randomness, so the same source always gives
                the same image. It then keeps, for each figure, a critique log under eight required headings:
                composition, contrast, visual hierarchy, spacing, accessibility, colour, label legibility at the size it
                is rendered and an overall verdict. The log grows with every pass instead of being overwritten:
                it keeps the history of what was seen and fixed. The sprezzature gallery holds one hundred and
                twenty-seven kinds of charts, every one of them put through this loop: that is its proving ground.
            </p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Who looks? By default, the agent that just
                wrote the code looks at its own output. An option hands the first pass to a vision model running
                offline, which a human then rereads. The difference matters, because a machine judge can be measured. A
                benchmark published in September 2026 took multimodal models, the ones that read images as well as text
                and put them to work judging charts. It finds systematic weaknesses in them, chief among them a strong
                tendency to accept everything<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>. The
                detail that matters for what follows: models rewarded during training
                when their answer pleases are the most lenient of all. The judge bends under the very pressure it is
                supposed to referee. All the same, it is reason enough to keep a human eye in the loop.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A concrete case says it better than a lot of
                theory. This one comes from this very page. The articles on this blog descend from a layout tool's
                export. The stylesheet, the file that fixes the page's appearance, declared a spacing between paragraphs
                that another rule reset to zero. The result: across the eight published articles, blocks of text were
                touching. Everything was valid, no test failed, no automated audit complained. Someone had to photograph
                the rendered page and look at it. The same pass surfaced two more defects of the same kind: bibliography
                numbers sitting on the first letter of their note and stray blank lines between list items. Three
                defects visible in one second of looking, invisible in any amount of reading the code.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The loop earns its keep where the judge sees
                what the actor cannot. A person with ordinary colour vision may take all the care in the world; they will never see their own figure the way a
                colour-blind reader sees it, because the information is not in their
                eye. A simulator redraws the image as those readers perceive it, then in grayscale. Grayscale is in fact
                the harshest test of all: a figure that reads in black and white reads under every milder deficiency.
                If the point survives grey, it survives everyone. And the criterion checks in a second, which makes it a
                stopping condition almost as cheap as comparing a counter to ten.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>Second material: prose
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Nothing in this discipline is specific to
                figures: change the judge in the stopping condition and the same skeleton applies to
                text. The
                writing charter this article answers to makes it a rule<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>. You do not reread a text paragraph by
                paragraph, but by pairs of consecutive paragraphs, because flow is not decided inside a block: it is
                decided at the seam. You read paragraph <em class="framer-text">n</em> followed by paragraph
                <em class="framer-text">n+1</em> aloud, in one go. You examine the crossing from one to the other. Then
                you
                fix the text itself: the order of sentences, the last line of the first, the first line of the second,
                instead of noting in the margin that "something sticks". Then you slide one notch, to the next pair, and the window starts over.
            </p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Because the windows overlap, each paragraph
                is tested twice, once as an arrival and once as a departure. Reading aloud plays the role of the render
                here: it turns a text you think you know, since you wrote it, into a signal you receive instead of
                emit, which is what this article went through.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Here too, the danger has been measured rather
                than feared. Jane Pan, He He, Samuel Bowman and Shi Feng showed in 2024 that in a loop where the same
                model writes and grades, the grade climbs while quality as judged by real readers stalls or falls; the
                effect is worst when writer and grader share their context<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-9">9</a></sup>. That is the sharpest argument for a reread by someone who did not write the text.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Reading aloud has a blind spot, though. It
                tests the seam between two paragraphs, which only shows at the crossing from one to the other. It
                does not test the inside of either. An ambiguous sentence you wrote yourself reads smoothly, because
                you already know what it means. The ear cannot catch what memory fills in. So a second instrument is
                needed, working on the same unit but looking elsewhere.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">That instrument is called ABABA and the name
                describes the operation. You write a passage in French, translate it into English, translate it back
                into French, then start again. A for one language, B for the other. ABABA therefore counts five
                states, four translations and two full rounds. The word is a palindrome: it reads the same in both
                directions. That is exactly the property being tested on the text.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Write \(T_{AB}\) for the translation into
                English and \(T_{BA}\) for the return trip. One full round is their composition
                \(R = T_{BA} \circ T_{AB}\). The sequence of successive versions is then:</p>
            <div class="drz-math framer-text">$$x_{n+1} \;=\; R(x_n)$$</div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A well-written passage is a fixed point of
                \(R\): it comes back from the trip intact, because its meaning is carried by its words and not by
                the reader's goodwill. Machine learning has a name for this property: cycle consistency. It took a
                hundred-year detour through paintings and horses before coming back to text.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The journey opens on a writer's revenge. In
                1903, Mark Twain published his jumping-frog story in three versions: his own, the French translation
                Thérèse Bentzon had made of it and his own word-for-word retranslation of that French back into
                English, French syntax and all<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-10">10</a></sup>.
                The result is grotesque, which is the point:
                Twain puts on display what the trip did to his text. The device turned serious in 1970, when Richard
                Brislin made it the standard quality control of cross-cultural research<sup class="drz-ref"><a
                        class="framer-text" href="#ref-11">11</a></sup>. You
                translate a questionnaire, you translate it back and the gap names the badly worded question before
                it skews a clinical study.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Next come the horses. In
                2017,
                Jun-Yan Zhu, Taesung Park, Phillip Isola and Alexei Efros set out to turn photographs of horses into
                zebras<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-12">12</a></sup>. The difficulty: no
                matching pairs of photographs exist, so there is no
                answer key to lean on. Their solution was to require that a round trip give back the starting image.
                Efros says where the idea came from; the answer closes the circle<sup class="drz-ref"><a
                        class="framer-text" href="#ref-13">13</a></sup>: from Mark
                Twain and from back-translation. He gives the version anyone understands. To have a text rendered
                into a language you do not know, you pay one translator for the outward leg and a second for the
                return, then you check that the thing still stands up. Born in text, the technique had left for images; we are bringing it home.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Efros also supplies the image that explains
                why
                a round trip teaches you anything. The setup is an autoencoder, the arrangement that compresses a
                piece of data then rebuilds it, except that here the narrow passage in the middle is not a
                compression: it is another language. The text is forced through a foreign representation, then back.
                That passage throws a great deal away. It throws away the turns of phrase that are ours without
                carrying meaning. It throws away the word order we had thought elegant. Above all, it cannot render
                an ambiguity: the translator has to pick one reading and fix it.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A sentence that comes back deformed therefore
                marks a place where the wording does not constrain the reading enough on its own. It may be an
                ambiguity. It may equally be under-specification, a turn of phrase the target language will not
                carry, a usage that differs between languages, or simply the translator's preference. The loop does
                not tell these apart: it points at the spot, and going to look is our job.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">It does see what we no longer can, since we
                already knew what we meant and the translator does not. It is the first naive reader anyone actually
                has on hand. An unglossed acronym, a term defined nowhere, a pronoun whose antecedent trails three
                sentences back: none of the three survives the round.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Stability alone proves nothing and Efros says
                so himself in the same talk. If all you ask is a return to the starting point, the network can cheat
                by not moving at all: the identity map cycles perfectly. A second constraint had to be added,
                demanding that a translated horse really look like a zebra. The measured version of that cheat
                arrived the same year. Casey Chu, Andrey Zhmoginov and Mark Sandler showed that CycleGAN learns to
                hide the source image inside an imperceptible noise added to its own
                output<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-14">14</a></sup>. The cycle closes
                flawlessly without anything having been translated. They
                called it steganography, the art of concealing a message inside an innocuous-looking carrier.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Hence a two-part criterion, neither part
                optional. <strong class="framer-text">Stability</strong> is measured against the previous round: is
                the text still moving? <strong class="framer-text">Fidelity</strong> is measured against the
                original, always, never against the previous round. In practice you list the claims made by the
                starting text, list those made by the arriving text, then compare the two lists. There is a reason
                for that asymmetry. A drift in meaning advances through links that are invisible one at a time and
                considerable once composed. It also conceals itself: by the second round the text has become
                coherent with its own error.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">This article's central trap returns here in a
                sharp form. \(R\) has an attractor, that is, a state the repetition pulls the text towards whatever its starting point. That attractor is the translator's average prose. Iterating
                to convergence means arriving there: flattened vocabulary, dead rhythm, bolted-on transitions. The
                loop clarifies and flattens in one motion, without being able to separate the two. A neighbouring
                phenomenon has been measured elsewhere. Ilia Shumailov and his coauthors showed in 2024 that a model
                retrained on its own outputs first loses the tails of its distribution, that is, whatever was rare in
                it<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-15">15</a></sup>. The analogy has a limit worth
                stating: their loop retrains the model, ours only
                queries it. What grows poorer here is not in the weights, it is in the text.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The remedy is simple: never keep the loop's output; keep as a map whatever moves from one round to the next. The passages that refuse to settle are the badly written ones;
                you rewrite those by hand. Instability is the signal; the fixed point is not. If a round turns
                "X" into "Y", the useful information is not Y. It is that X was rewritable, so a reader could read it
                as Y. That was all we wanted to know. What comes next is written in our own hand, which protects the
                register.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Alternating the translators costs little;
                Twain and Efros already prescribed it by
                hiring two different people. With a
                single model the fixed point is its own style; with two models in alternation, a text that settles has
                to satisfy both models' preferences rather than one's, which pulls it away from either one's habits.
                The picture is an intuition, not a proved property of the system. The language you pass through deserves the same care. French and English share too much
                Latin machinery: a calque slips across unresisted and a badly built French sentence stays badly
                built in English without anything sounding wrong. For diagnosis, a structurally distant language such
                as Japanese or Finnish forces a real re-encoding.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A serious objection belongs here rather than
                under the rug. Harold Somers showed in 2005 that the round trip is a poor way to grade a machine
                translator<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-16">16</a></sup>: errors on the way out
                and errors on the way back cancel each other, so a
                successful round proves nothing about the quality of either translation. The objection is sound and
                does not apply here, because the unknown has moved. Somers varies the translator and holds the text
                fixed, since he wants to grade the translator. We do the opposite: the translator is held fixed and
                assumed good; it is the text that varies.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Two recent measurements confirm that the
                thing
                can be measured when approached this way. Jihyung Moon, Hyunchang Cho and Eunjeong Park showed in
                2020 that the round trip predicts quality well, provided you compare meanings rather than
                words<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-17">17</a></sup>. In April 2026, Ronald
                Skorobogat, Ameya Prabhu and Matthias Bethge measured a
                correlation of 0.94 between a round-trip score and the preferences of real users, where two
                established multilingual benchmarks fall to zero or turn
                negative<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-18">18</a></sup>. The error cancellation
                Somers describes remains a good reason never to
                conclude from a single round with a single model.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Two instruments, then, on the same unit of
                text. The ear tests the seam, translation tests the inside. You read aloud first, because it is free
                and settles half the problems. You launch the round trip only on the pairs that resist and never
                more than two rounds on a passage that yields. Past five, you are measuring the attractor and not the
                text.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>The trap: the judge ends up bought
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A loop optimizes what it measures. Put a
                judge into the stopping condition and the loop starts optimizing the judge. Charles Goodhart observed
                it in 1975 on British monetary policy: a statistical regularity collapses as soon as it is used as a
                target<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-19">19</a></sup>. The anthropologist
                Marilyn Strathern gave it in 1997 the wording that stuck, while
                studying university assessment: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good
                measure<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-20">20</a></sup>. The modern, measured
                version, on language models: Leo Gao, John Schulman and Jacob
                Hilton optimized harder and harder against an artificial judge; the awarded score climbs without pause,
                while true quality climbs, plateaus, then falls<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-21">21</a></sup>. The judge does not break at once: it bends as
                you push, which is far harder to notice.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">In a Ralph loop, that corruption can be
                priced. Take a forecasting model being improved by iterations. On each turn, the agent proposes a
                change; you measure it on a validation set, data held out on the side, never used for training and only
                for arbitration. That measurement is noisy, like every measurement made on a finite sample: redone on
                other data, it would move. Call \(\sigma\) its standard error, the typical size of those fluctuations.
                The ideas, for their part, have real effects, sometimes good, sometimes bad, with typical spread
                \(\tau\). You try \(K\) ideas and keep the one that measures best.</p>
            <div class="drz-art-fig drz-art-fig--wide"><img src="<?= ASSETS ?>/images/blog/ralph-curse-en.svg"
                    alt="Two curves against the number of ideas tried: the gain read on validation climbs to 3.32 standard errors, the real gain obtained only to 0.27."
                    loading="lazy">
                <p class="framer-text drz-art-figcap">Two hundred thousand simulated draws, with measurement noise
                    three and a half times the spread of the true effects. The upper curve is what validation
                    announces; the lower one is what survives being measured elsewhere.</p>
            </div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The simulation above takes measurement noise
                three and a half times larger than the gap between a good idea and a bad one, which is nothing
                extravagant in practice. After eight hundred ideas tried, the winning change shows a validation gain of
                3.32 standard errors. Its true gain is 0.27: twelve times less. The gap between the two curves is
                exactly what going to production always claws back.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Nothing accidental there. The intuition fits
                a schoolroom scene: the student who takes the top mark in a competitive exam has, in general, both
                ability and luck, because selecting the best paper also selects the luck. The best estimate of their
                true level is therefore a little below their mark. The law that prices this discount fits in a formula:
                given a measurement \(m\), the best estimate of the true effect \(\theta\) behind it is not \(m\) but
                a version shrunk toward zero, the more so as the measurement is noisy:</p>
            <div class="drz-math framer-text">$$\mathbb{E}[\theta \mid m] \;=\; \frac{\tau^2}{\tau^2 + \sigma^2}\; m$$</div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">This is the Bayesian shrinkage formula, from
                the same family of ideas as the estimators popularized by James and Stein in the
                1950s<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-22">22</a></sup> and which Bradley Efron
                tied in 2011 to selection bias itself<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-23">23</a></sup>: a value picked
                because it was the largest systematically overstates what it measures. With the simulation's values,
                the factor is 12.1: whatever validation announces, divide it by twelve. The number of ideas tried does
                not change that factor; it changes the size of the mirage, which grows like \(\sqrt{2\ln K}\), a
                function that climbs very slowly but never stops. A loop left running therefore ends up announcing an
                arbitrarily large gain on its judge, without having improved anything. The same mechanism explains why
                so many published results fail to replicate, as John Ioannidis showed in 2005<sup class="drz-ref"><a
                        class="framer-text" href="#ref-24">24</a></sup>. Petroleum
                economists had named it as early as 1971, observing that the winner of an auction is the bidder who most
                overestimated the tract: the winner's curse<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-25">25</a></sup>.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">One limit, stated plainly: this calculation
                is a simulation assuming independent ideas with no average talent. That is pessimistic for a competent
                engineer, whose proposals are not drawn at random; optimistic for an agent producing twenty variants
                of the same idea. It is a model of the mechanism, not a verdict on any particular loop. What survives
                the caveats fits in one sentence: what you read exceeds what you get; that gap widens with every extra
                try.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>The same thing, on real data
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A simulation is still a simulation. Here is
                the same experiment on real data; it is harsher than the model. Eight hundred images of handwritten
                digits, cut in two. Four hundred serve the judge, a cross-validation whose workings the next section
                explains; the other four hundred form a frozen test the loop never sees. On each turn, the loop proposes
                a small change to the input variables, accepts it if the validation score rises, rejects it otherwise.
                Two hundred proposals examined, ten accepted. The full protocol is given so it can be redone rather than
                believed, seed included. The seed is the number that pins down the randomness: anyone rerunning the
                program gets exactly the same experiment. The <code class="framer-text">load_digits</code> dataset from
                scikit-learn, seed 20260922, logistic regression, five-fold cross-validation.</p>
            <div class="drz-art-fig drz-art-fig--wide"><img src="<?= ASSETS ?>/images/blog/ralph-real-en.svg"
                    alt="Two step curves over two hundred turns: validation climbs in steps to over three points, the frozen test ends half a point down."
                    loading="lazy">
                <p class="framer-text drz-art-figcap">Every step of the upper curve is a change accepted because it had
                    raised validation. The lower curve says what those ten decisions were worth.</p>
            </div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Validation gains three points, in ten steps,
                each one justified by a measured rise. The frozen test loses half a point. The whole announced progress
                was noise the loop chased. The important point is not that the loop failed here: it is that it failed
                exactly the way it would have succeeded. Its validation curve climbs in steps, like the curve of a loop
                that is genuinely winning. From the inside, the two situations cannot be told apart, try as you might. Only a frozen set
                separates them, consulted once.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A second objection weighs more than the
                first. The largest empirical check points the other way: Rebecca Roelofs and her coauthors
                compared, across more than a hundred Kaggle competitions, the public leaderboards, built from thousands
                of submissions, against the private leaderboards revealed once at the end; they found little
                overfitting<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-29">29</a></sup>. The explanation fits
                in two words: effective multiplicity. Twenty variants of one
                idea do not count as twenty independent tries. The handwritten-digits experiment draws nearly
                independent tries: it is the unfavourable case, chosen as such. The useful question is therefore not
                whether a loop overfits, but how many genuinely distinct tries it has made.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>The budget or what makes a number believable
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Should you then give up searching in a loop?
                No; a comparison shows why. The classic reflex for tuning a model is the grid: enumerate
                combinations of settings fixed in advance, evaluate them all, keep the best. James Bergstra and Yoshua
                Bengio showed in 2012 that at equal budget, drawing the combinations at random already beats a regular
                grid<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-26">26</a></sup>. A Ralph loop goes further:
                its next proposal depends on what the previous measurement
                said. Statisticians call that adaptive analysis and distrust it for good reason, because a validation
                set wears out much faster under questions that depend on the answers already obtained; Cynthia Dwork
                and her coauthors built quantified guarantees for that case<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-27">27</a></sup>. You would therefore expect the loop
                to do worse than the grid. The simulation says the opposite.</p>
            <div class="drz-art-fig drz-art-fig--wide"><img src="<?= ASSETS ?>/images/blog/ralph-grid-en.svg"
                    alt="True gain against the number of evaluations: the non-adaptive grid plateaus at 0.27 standard errors, the adaptive loop reaches 1.64."
                    loading="lazy">
                <p class="framer-text drz-art-figcap">Same judge, same evaluation budget, two ways of searching. The
                    grid draws every candidate from scratch; the loop restarts each time from the previous winner.</p>
            </div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">At equal budget, the grid obtains a true gain
                of 0.27 standard errors, the loop 1.64: six times more. Where does the advantage come from? A third
                experiment settles it; it plays the role of the control group in a drug trial. The same step-by-step
                walk, but accepting everything without ever looking at the measurement, brings nothing: so it is the
                selection that does the work, not the accumulation alone. Conversely, the same walk judged by an oracle
                that could see the true effects would gain sixty times more: the loop, with its noisy judge, recovers
                only two percent of what was available. As for the mirage, it is identical on both sides: grid and loop
                overstate their gain by the same amount. The inflation does not depend on how you search, only on how
                many times you consulted the judge.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">From this comes a budget rule, whose address
                deserves to be given precisely, because it is not mine. In 2014, David Bailey, Jonathan Borwein, Marcos
                López de Prado and Qiji Zhu treated this exact problem for financial backtests, the simulations that
                replay an investment strategy on the past<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-28">28</a></sup>. Their result: below a certain amount of data, a
                flattering track record proves nothing; with five years of history, you should not try more than
                forty-five independent configurations. Carried over to a loop that tolerates an optimism of \(\delta\)
                standard errors, the same bound gives a cautious rule:</p>
            <div class="drz-math framer-text">$$K \;\le\; \exp\!\left(\frac{\delta^{2}}{2}\right)$$</div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Cautious, because the formula overshoots: the
                exact calculation allows about three tries for a tolerance of one standard error, twenty-seven for two,
                four hundred and forty-three for three, where the formula concedes only one, seven and ninety. And
                \(K\) must be counted in genuinely distinct tries, not in loop turns. What survives every caveat, and
                has been proven since 2014 in an entirely different field, fits in one sentence: capping the number of
                turns is not a compute saving, it is the condition for the final number to mean anything. A loop without
                a budget ends up reporting what you hoped to hear.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">What really tips the scale toward the loop is
                not the factor of six anyway. It is that a grid assumes you can list the candidates in advance. The
                changes that matter cannot be listed: adding a derived variable, changing the loss function,
                fixing a data leak. You cannot put those in a grid, for want of being able to write its axes. The loop
                searches where there is no grid to draw: that is its irreplaceable advantage. The rest has a price.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>The everyday judge: cross-validation
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Everything above rests on \(\sigma\), the
                judge's noise. In a real project, that quantity gets set without anyone noticing, at the moment the data
                split is chosen. The standard tool is cross-validation: split the dataset into \(k\) parts, the folds;
                train on \(k-1\) folds, measure on the last and start again \(k\) times, changing the held-out fold
                each time. You then quote the mean of the \(k\) scores, with an error bar.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">That bar is almost always too optimistic, for
                a simple reason: the calculation treats the \(k\) measurements as independent, when two folds share
                nearly all their training data. Gaël Varoquaux documented the size of the problem: on small samples,
                published bars are routinely too narrow by half<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-30">30</a></sup>. On the handwritten-digits example, the
                variability that matters, from one sample to the next, is nearly double the between-fold spread everyone
                quotes. Underestimating \(\sigma\) by a factor of two means underestimating \(\sigma^{2}\) by a factor
                of four, hence believing in four times more progress than there is. The split is not a logistical
                detail: it sets the exchange rate between what you read and what you get.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">That is what the tool suite published by
                probabl around scikit-learn, skrub and skore codifies<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-31">31</a></sup>: a split by groups when several rows come
                from the same subject, a chronological split with a buffer zone when time order matters and an explicit
                warning against the habits that produce overconfident bars. The suite reads like a Ralph loop written
                out in full: state lives in a logbook, the judge is a report distinct from whoever wrote the pipeline,
                every proposal arrives with its written reason and several steps stop dead rather than carry on over a
                missing dependency.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">One detail there deserves copying everywhere.
                Its smoke test is not a judgement, it is an exact assertion: the number of predictions must equal the
                number of rows to predict. It catches one precise, frequent fault. There is a judgement turned back into
                a cheap test. Every Ralph loop gains from manufacturing as many of these as it can: whatever the machine
                can settle has no business sitting in the expensive condition.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>Forecasting without cheating
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">This section describes a project just
                starting, proposed to two students of the specialized master's in artificial intelligence at
                CentraleSupélec by Marianne Clausel, of the Université de Lorraine, Georges Oppenheim and myself. It
                concerns forecasting financial series; its evaluation protocol is called LookForward. The rule is short:
                at each date you forecast from, the origin, the model uses only the information available on that date,
                predicts the next steps, then the origin advances one notch. A forecast evaluated on data it has already
                seen is not a forecast, it is an interpolation. The difficulty is not in the model, it is in the
                calendar: an indicator published on the fifteenth cannot serve a forecast made on the tenth.</p>
            <div class="drz-art-fig drz-art-fig--wide"><img src="<?= ASSETS ?>/images/blog/ralph-lookforward-en.svg"
                    alt="Four successive origins: on each row a zone of available information, three forecast cells, a forbidden zone and the origin advancing by one cell; below, the frozen final chronological test."
                    loading="lazy">
                <p class="framer-text drz-art-figcap">LookForward. The judge is not a randomly drawn dataset but a
                    discipline of the calendar: no row ever reads to the right of its origin.</p>
            </div>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Finance is the very field where Bailey and
                his coauthors showed that with enough tries, you get a flattering track record for any strategy,
                even one with no predictive power at all<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-28">28</a></sup>. Hence the protocol's shape. A Ralph loop under
                budget tests each change in rolling validation, then accepts or rejects it while recording the
                reason. Kept apart from all of it, a final chronological test, frozen, opened once, yields
                the number that will be published. The budget caps the \(K\) of the formula; the recorded reason makes
                it auditable; the frozen test keeps the factor of twelve from slipping by unnoticed.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The project compares three families:
                classical
                statistical methods, machine-learning methods and time-series foundation models, those large models
                already trained on huge collections of series and reused as they are. It follows the open protocol
                TFB<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-32">32</a></sup>, with Chronos
                and TimesFM among the candidates<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-33">33</a>,&nbsp;<a class="framer-text" href="#ref-34">34</a></sup>. Nothing in it
                presumes that a recent model beats the most naive
                forecast there is: tomorrow will be worth what today is worth. It finally allows itself a rare exit:
                abstention, backed by conformal risk control<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-35">35</a></sup>, whose guarantee rests on an assumption that
                financial series strain; the project sheet says so itself. It is the same restraint that
                <a class="framer-text" href="https://deraison.ai/en/blog/elbow-helper">elbow-helper</a> applies to curve
                elbows: better to stay silent than to assert without proof.
            </p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">One detail of the deliverables sums up
                everything above. The sheet asks that the figures' legibility be audited with the Eyeball approach,
                separately from the numerical validation of the forecasts. Two different questions, two different
                judges. Confusing them means asking a score to tell you whether a chart is readable or an eye to tell
                you whether an interval covers.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>One cycle across four materials
            </h2>
            <div class="drz-table-wrap">
                <table class="drz-doc-table drz-doc-table--words">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Loop</th>
                            <th>What gets rendered</th>
                            <th>Who judges</th>
                            <th>How the judge gets bought</th>
                            <th>The guard</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Code</th>
                            <td>The repository after one turn</td>
                            <td>The test suite, then a human</td>
                            <td>Tests become the target; code passes without being right</td>
                            <td>Tests written first, by someone else</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Figures</th>
                            <td>An image, always the same for a given source</td>
                            <td>An eye or a vision model</td>
                            <td>You get used to your own figure and stop seeing it</td>
                            <td>Grayscale, colour-vision simulation, a fresh pair of eyes</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Prose</th>
                            <td>The text read aloud, then translated and translated back</td>
                            <td>The ear for the seam, a translator for the inside</td>
                            <td>The text converges on the translator's average prose</td>
                            <td>Keep the diff and not the output, alternate translators</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Forecasting</th>
                            <td>Dated errors, horizon by horizon</td>
                            <td>A rolling validation</td>
                            <td>The more you consult a validation set, the more it wears out</td>
                            <td>A budget, a recorded reason, a frozen test</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p class="framer-text drz-art-figcap">One cycle across four materials. The fourth column is the same
                sentence every time, spoken in four languages.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>Where the idea comes from and what this article adds
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">A clarification, because the word is starting
                to mean everything. Iterating through production, critique and correction is in no way specific to Ralph
                and predates it: Self-Refine has the model critique itself<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-36">36</a></sup>; Reflexion keeps a memory of comments
                from one attempt to the next<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-37">37</a></sup>.
                What is specific to Huntley's technique is a combination: a fresh
                context on every turn rather than a lengthening conversation, durable state outside the model, real
                execution of the code produced, one single thing done per turn. As for the loops on prose, figures and
                forecasting described above, they are my generalization of the pattern, not an established sense of the
                term: a reader searching for "Ralph loop" elsewhere will find code and nothing else.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">None of the numbered part is mine either.
                Quantitative finance proved in 2014 that selecting among \(N\) backtests imposes a minimum length of
                history<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text" href="#ref-28">28</a></sup>. Theoretical computer
                science proved in 2015 that a held-out set only survives a
                bounded number of adaptive questions<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-27">27</a></sup>. The alignment community measured, between 2022 and 2024,
                that pushing too hard against a stand-in judge destroys real quality<sup class="drz-ref"><a
                        class="framer-text" href="#ref-21">21</a></sup>. Shrinkage toward the mean
                dates from the 1950s. Meanwhile, in 2025, software engineering put into production a loop with no
                stopping condition at all. These literatures do not cite one another. This article's only contribution
                is to say that they describe the same object and that the budget rule holds for agent harnesses exactly
                as it does for market strategies.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>The guards
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">The cycle comes with four guards, checked
                before anything is launched. State lives outside the model, in files and version history, never in a
                conversation. The judge is independent of the actor: the eye that evaluates is not the hand that drew,
                the validation set is not the training set. A budget caps the number of turns. Every turn leaves a written reason, lest the \(K\) of the formula be lost and the final gain with it. And
                abstention is a legal exit, on the same footing as success: a loop that is not allowed to conclude it
                found nothing will always find something.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Those four guards amount to a working
                definition, one that fits on a line and serves better than the word "loop" on its own. A good agent
                loop is not a "retry until it passes". It is an adaptive search with persistent state, under an
                outside oracle, with an explicit budget and a right to abstain. Each of the four terms answers to one
                of the guards, and dropping any one of them makes the final number unverifiable.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">They also obey one principle, which
                Efros puts better than I can in the talk already cited<sup class="drz-ref"><a class="framer-text"
                        href="#ref-13">13</a></sup>. You do not build an
                incorruptible judge: no such thing exists. The data is finite, so there will always be some path for
                cheating; plugging one hole opens another. What you can do is arrange the problem so that
                cheating costs more than working. He uses the image of a well-run country: you can break the law
                there, you simply have no interest in doing so. In a badly run country, cheating is the rational
                calculation and everyone cheats.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">His schoolroom example comes from his own
                classroom. A whole semester of work is expensive; memorising last year's past papers is cheap, yet it
                often suffices to pass the exam. The student who does this is not dishonest, only rational: it is the
                exam that is badly built. A validation set consulted two hundred times is exactly those past papers.
                The budget, the written reason and the frozen test do not moralise the loop; they raise the price of
                cheating until working becomes the cheapest road again.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">One last thing: where the analogy with the
                while loop finally gives way. A classic loop that fails to stop is noticed immediately: the machine
                heats up
                and nothing comes out. A Ralph loop that fails to stop produces plausible work on every turn, a slightly
                different figure, a slightly reworked paragraph, a slightly better score. It does not look like a
                breakdown. It looks like progress. That is exactly what makes it dangerous.</p>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Hence the closing recipe: render what you
                just made, actually look at it, fix the source and not the image, count your turns. The first three
                steps cost seconds. The fourth is the one everyone forgets and the only one the other three depend on.
                Because an autonomous loop is not worth the quality of its actor: it is worth the evaluation
                architecture it is forbidden to overfit.</p>
            <h2 dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-yezh60">
                <br>Bibliography
            </h2>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Every title below went into writing one
                particular section; the note says which. None is cited second-hand.</p>
            <ol class="drz-biblio framer-text">
                <li id="ref-1" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Huntley, G. (2025). <em
                            class="framer-text">Ralph Wiggum as a &quot;software engineer&quot;</em>. <a
                            class="framer-text" href="https://ghuntley.com/ralph/" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">ghuntley.com/ralph</a></cite> <span class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">The
                        page that names the technique and gives its shell line. The author also states the caveats
                        quoted here: greenfield only, a senior engineer required, output whose doneness is not
                        guaranteed. It carries no stopping condition at all: the operator decides. The cost figure cited
                        is his.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-2" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Huntley, G. (2026). <em
                            class="framer-text">everything is a ralph loop</em>. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://ghuntley.com/loop/" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">ghuntley.com/loop</a></cite> <span class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Argues
                        the pattern holds for all tasks and sketches several orchestration variants. It is the most
                        direct antecedent of the generalization defended here; generality is asserted rather than
                        structured, with no common skeleton and no failure-mode analysis.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-3" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Barr, E.&nbsp;T., Harman, M., McMinn, P.,
                        Shahbaz, M. and Yoo, S. (2015). "The Oracle Problem in Software Testing: A Survey," <em
                            class="framer-text">IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering</em>, 41(5), 507-525. <a
                            class="framer-text" href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2014.2372785" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">10.1109/TSE.2014.2372785</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Surveys thirty years of attempts to decide automatically
                        whether observed behaviour is correct and concludes that where specifications, models and
                        metamorphic testing stop, the last source of oracle information remains the human. This is
                        software engineering's statement of the problem, long predating agents.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-4" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Huang, J., Chen, X., Mishra, S., Zheng,
                        H.&nbsp;S., Yu, A.&nbsp;W., Song, X. and Zhou, D. (2024). <em class="framer-text">Large Language
                            Models Cannot Self-Correct Reasoning Yet</em>, ICLR. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01798" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2310.01798</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Separates self-correction from the model's own resources
                        alone from correction leaning on an outside signal and shows the former fails, performance
                        sometimes degrading after correction. It is the empirical case for the judge coming from
                        somewhere other than the actor.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-5" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Chen, M., Wang, L. and Qu, B. (2026). <em
                            class="framer-text">Recursive Self-Improvement in AI: From Bounded Self-Refinement to
                            Autonomous Research Loops</em>. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07663" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2607.07663</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">A survey of 1,250 papers holding that every improvement loop
                        is a claim that some signal can substitute for human judgement and ranking those signals from
                        formal verifier to intrinsic self-assessment. It is the closest statement to this article's
                        framing and it came first of the two by several months.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-6" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Harchaoui, W. <em class="framer-text">The
                            Ralph Eyeball Loop</em>, Sprezzature. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://sprezzature.ai/ralph-eyeball-loop.html" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">sprezzature.ai/ralph-eyeball-loop.html</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Applies the loop to visual artifacts and separates three
                        complementary passes: make it from code, audit the source, look at the render. It is the source
                        of the four steps, the eight critique headings and the argument for grayscale as the severest
                        test.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-7" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Wu, L., Zhao, H.&nbsp;H., Zhang, Z., Tang,
                        J., Wu, J. and Wang, A.&nbsp;J. (2026). <em class="framer-text">ChartJudgeBench: Evaluating LMM
                            Judges for Chart-to-Code Generation</em>. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24210" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2609.24210</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Evaluates multimodal models used as chart judges and
                        documents four systematic weaknesses, among them a marked tendency to accept and greater
                        leniency in reinforcement-trained models. It is the measurement of the visual judge the figure
                        loop depends on.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-8" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Harchaoui, W. <em
                            class="framer-text">WRITING.md</em>, a writing charter for English prose. <a
                            class="framer-text"
                            href="https://gist.github.com/warith-harchaoui/f45304d066abc81dd7d4f059a1f4e45f"
                            target="_blank" rel="noopener">gist.github.com</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Section 10 carries the loop over to prose: rereading by
                        pairs of consecutive paragraphs, overlapping sliding windows, fixing the text rather than the
                        impression. It is the charter this article applies to itself.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-9" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Pan, J., He, H., Bowman, S.&nbsp;R. and
                        Feng, S. (2024). <em class="framer-text">Spontaneous Reward Hacking in Iterative
                            Self-Refinement</em>. <a class="framer-text" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.04549"
                            target="_blank" rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2407.04549</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Shows that in a loop where one model both writes and grades,
                        the grade rises while quality as perceived by real readers stalls or falls, the more so when
                        writer and grader share context. The failure mode described here was therefore already named and
                        measured on prose.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-10" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Twain, M. (1903). <em
                            class="framer-text">The Jumping Frog: In English, Then in French, Then Clawed Back into a
                            Civilized Language Once More by Patient, Unremunerated Toil</em>, Harper &amp;
                        Brothers.</cite> <span class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Twain prints his story, the French
                        translation Thérèse Bentzon made of it and his own word-for-word retranslation, side by side.
                        The demonstration is comic and exact: the round trip makes visible whatever was fragile in the
                        text. It is the literary ancestor of the loop described here.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-11" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Brislin, R.&nbsp;W. (1970).
                        "Back-Translation for Cross-Cultural Research", <em class="framer-text">Journal of
                            Cross-Cultural Psychology</em>, 1(3), 185-216. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1177/135910457000100301" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">10.1177/135910457000100301</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Establishes back-translation as quality control in
                        cross-cultural research; it has been the standard procedure ever since for translating a
                        clinical or psychometric questionnaire, where one lost nuance skews a whole study. It settles
                        that the return gap is an exploitable signal rather than noise.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-12" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Zhu, J.-Y., Park, T., Isola, P. and Efros,
                        A.&nbsp;A. (2017). "Unpaired Image-to-Image Translation using Cycle-Consistent Adversarial
                        Networks", <em class="framer-text">ICCV</em>, 2242-2251. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.10593" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arXiv:1703.10593</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">CycleGAN: with no matching image pairs available, the
                        training constraint becomes the round trip itself. It is the formalization of cycle consistency
                        used here, together with the proof that it can stand in for a missing answer key.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-13" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Efros, A.&nbsp;A. (2018). <em
                            class="framer-text">Self-supervision, Meta-supervision, Curiosity: Making Computers Study
                            Harder</em>, lecture at CIIRC, Czech Technical University in Prague, 25&nbsp;May 2018. <a
                            class="framer-text" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_V-WpE8cmpc" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">youtube.com/watch?v=_V-WpE8cmpc</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Efros explicitly credits CycleGAN's idea to Mark Twain and
                        back-translation and describes the setup as an autoencoder whose narrow passage is another
                        language (at 48&nbsp;min). He also states the limit taken up here: without a second constraint,
                        the network cheats by not moving. And the formulation that carries the end of this article: make
                        cheating more expensive than doing the work.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-14" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Chu, C., Zhmoginov, A. and Sandler, M.
                        (2017). <em class="framer-text">CycleGAN, a Master of Steganography</em>, "Machine Deception"
                        workshop, NIPS. <a class="framer-text" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.02950" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arXiv:1712.02950</a></cite> <span class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Shows
                        that CycleGAN conceals the source image in an imperceptible high-frequency noise, which lets it
                        reconstruct exactly without having translated anything. It is the demonstration that cycle
                        consistency alone is degenerate. Hence fidelity is measured here against the original, never
                        against the previous round.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-15" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Shumailov, I., Shumaylov, Z., Zhao, Y.,
                        Papernot, N., Anderson, R. and Gal, Y. (2024). "AI models collapse when trained on recursively
                        generated data", <em class="framer-text">Nature</em>, 631, 755-759. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07566-y" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">10.1038/s41586-024-07566-y</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Measures what becomes of a model retrained on its own
                        outputs: the tails of the distribution go first, which is to say whatever was rare. Cited here
                        as a measured analogue of the attractor, with its limit stated plainly: theirs is a retraining
                        loop, not a querying loop.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-16" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Somers, H. (2005). "Round-trip
                        Translation: What Is It Good For?", <em class="framer-text">Australasian Language Technology
                            Workshop</em>, 127-133. <a class="framer-text" href="https://aclanthology.org/U05-1019/"
                            target="_blank" rel="noopener">aclanthology.org/U05-1019</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">The substantive objection: the round trip grades a machine
                        translator very poorly, since errors on the outward and return legs cancel. Taken up here rather
                        than set aside; it does not target the same use, but it directly motivates alternating
                        translators.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-17" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Moon, J., Cho, H. and Park, E.&nbsp;L.
                        (2020). "Revisiting Round-Trip Translation for Quality Estimation", <em
                            class="framer-text">EAMT</em>. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.13937" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arXiv:2004.13937</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Rehabilitates the round trip by changing what gets compared:
                        meaning embeddings instead of word overlap. It is the technical answer to Somers, hence the
                        justification for the fidelity criterion used here, which bears on claims and not on
                        form.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-18" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Skorobogat, R., Prabhu, A. and Bethge, M.
                        (2026). <em class="framer-text">Round-Trip Translation Reveals What Frontier Multilingual
                            Benchmarks Miss</em>, 14&nbsp;April 2026. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12911" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arXiv:2604.12911</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Measures a correlation of 0.94 between a round-trip score
                        and user preferences, where two established multilingual benchmarks fall to −0.09 and −0.26. It
                        is the most recent evidence that the round trip catches something real. It bears on models
                        rather than on text, so it supports the use made here without proving it.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-19" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Goodhart, C.&nbsp;A.&nbsp;E. (1975).
                        "Problems of Monetary Management: The U.K. Experience," <em class="framer-text">Papers in
                            Monetary Economics</em>, Reserve Bank of Australia.</cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">The original observation, made on British monetary
                        aggregates: a statistical regularity stops holding once it is used as a policy target. It
                        grounds the section on the corruption of the judge.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-20" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Strathern, M. (1997). "'Improving
                        ratings': audit in the British University system," <em class="framer-text">European Review</em>,
                        5(3), 305-321.</cite> <span class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Supplies the short formulation
                        that became proverbial, out of ethnographic fieldwork on university assessment. Cited for the
                        exact wording rather than through a secondhand quotation.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-21" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Gao, L., Schulman, J. and Hilton, J.
                        (2023). "Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization," <em class="framer-text">ICML</em>,
                        PMLR 202, 10835-10866. <a class="framer-text" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.10760"
                            target="_blank" rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2210.10760</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Has a reward model stand in for the human judge and measures
                        what happens as you optimize against it: the score climbs, true quality plateaus then falls,
                        along a fitted functional form. Goodhart's law made quantitative, on a material this article
                        does not otherwise treat.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-22" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Efron, B. and Morris, C. (1977). "Stein's
                        Paradox in Statistics," <em class="framer-text">Scientific American</em>, 236(5),
                        119-127.</cite> <span class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">An accessible account of shrinkage
                        toward the mean: a value picked because it was the largest overstates what it measures. How much
                        to take back depends on the ratio of noise to real spread. That is the boxed formula in this
                        article. It dates from the 1950s.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-23" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Efron, B. (2011). "Tweedie's Formula and
                        Selection Bias," <em class="framer-text">Journal of the American Statistical Association</em>,
                        106(496), 1602-1614. <a class="framer-text" href="https://doi.org/10.1198/jasa.2011.tm11181"
                            target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.1198/jasa.2011.tm11181</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Ties shrinkage explicitly to selection bias: the largest few
                        estimates overstate their parameters, with the correction written from the observed marginal
                        density. It is the exact address of the factor used here.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-24" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Ioannidis, J.&nbsp;P.&nbsp;A. (2005). "Why
                        Most Published Research Findings Are False," <em class="framer-text">PLoS Medicine</em>, 2(8),
                        e124. <a class="framer-text" href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.0020124" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">10.1371/journal.pmed.0020124</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Makes quantitative the link between the number of hypotheses
                        explored and the probability that a reported result is false. Same mechanism as the one
                        simulated here, applied to the scientific literature.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-25" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Capen, E.&nbsp;C., Clapp, R.&nbsp;V. and
                        Campbell, W.&nbsp;M. (1971). "Competitive Bidding in High-Risk Situations," <em
                            class="framer-text">Journal of Petroleum Technology</em>, 23(6), 641-653.</cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Origin of the phrase "winner's curse," on auctions for oil
                        tracts: the winner is the bidder whose estimate was highest, hence most wrong. The same
                        structure as selection on a validation set.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-26" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Bergstra, J. and Bengio, Y. (2012).
                        "Random Search for Hyper-Parameter Optimization," <em class="framer-text">Journal of Machine
                            Learning Research</em>, 13, 281-305. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://jmlr.org/papers/v13/bergstra12a.html" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">jmlr.org/papers/v13/bergstra12a</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Shows that at equal budget, drawing settings at random beats
                        a regular grid, because performance depends strongly on only a few settings and a grid repeats
                        its trials on the others. It is the comparison baseline for the model-selection section.</span>
                </li>
                <li id="ref-27" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Dwork, C., Feldman, V., Hardt, M.,
                        Pitassi, T., Reingold, O. and Roth, A. (2015). "The reusable holdout: Preserving validity in
                        adaptive data analysis," <em class="framer-text">Science</em>, 349(6248), 636-638. <a
                            class="framer-text" href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaa9375" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">10.1126/science.aaa9375</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Separates questions posed in advance from questions
                        depending on answers already obtained and builds a mechanism restoring quantified validity to a
                        holdout under adaptive querying, at the cost of an explicit budget of disagreements. It is the
                        theoretical-computer-science proof that the budget is a necessity, not a prudence.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-28" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Bailey, D.&nbsp;H., Borwein, J.&nbsp;M.,
                        López de Prado, M. and Zhu, Q.&nbsp;J. (2014). "Pseudo-Mathematics and Financial Charlatanism:
                        The Effects of Backtest Overfitting on Out-of-Sample Performance," <em
                            class="framer-text">Notices of the American Mathematical Society</em>, 61(5), 458-471. <a
                            class="framer-text" href="https://doi.org/10.1090/noti1105" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">10.1090/noti1105</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Computes the expected maximum of \(N\) skill-free trials,
                        bounds its growth by \(\sqrt{2\ln N}\) and derives a minimum-backtest-length theorem. It is the
                        source of this article's budget rule, which only carries it across; the precedence is total, the
                        bound itself coming from extreme-value theory.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-29" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Roelofs, R., Shankar, V., Recht, B.,
                        Fridovich-Keil, S., Hardt, M., Miller, J. and Schmidt, L. (2019). <em class="framer-text">A
                            Meta-Analysis of Overfitting in Machine Learning</em>, NeurIPS.</cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Compares, across more than a hundred Kaggle competitions,
                        public leaderboards built from thousands of submissions against private leaderboards revealed
                        once: the observed overfitting is small, if anything. It is the empirical counter-evidence to the worst-case
                        theory and the reason to speak of effective multiplicity rather than of turn count.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-30" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Varoquaux, G. (2018). "Cross-validation
                        failure: Small sample sizes lead to large error bars," <em class="framer-text">NeuroImage</em>,
                        180, 68-77. <a class="framer-text" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2017.06.061"
                            target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.1016/j.neuroimage.2017.06.061</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Shows the standard deviation across folds strongly
                        underestimates the real uncertainty of a cross-validated estimate, enough to cast doubt on
                        published comparisons, with bars around ten points for a hundred samples. It justifies the
                        decomposition measured here.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-31" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">probabl (2026). <em
                            class="framer-text">skills</em>, agent skills for scikit-learn, skrub and skore,
                        BSD-3-Clause. <a class="framer-text" href="https://github.com/probabl-ai/skills" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">github.com/probabl-ai/skills</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">A suite chaining exploration, construction, smoke test,
                        evaluation and audit, with an outer loop that sources the next experiment. It serves here as an
                        example of a Ralph loop whose judge and stop conditions are written down, notably its decision
                        tree for choosing a splitter.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-32" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Qiu, X. et al. (2024). <em
                            class="framer-text">TFB: Towards Comprehensive and Fair Benchmarking of Time Series
                            Forecasting Methods</em>. <a class="framer-text" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.20150"
                            target="_blank" rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2403.20150</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">An open benchmarking protocol for time-series forecasting,
                        built to avoid comparisons skewed by uneven tuning. It is the evaluation frame the project
                        described here adopts.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-33" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Ansari, A.&nbsp;F. et al. (2024). <em
                            class="framer-text">Chronos: Learning the Language of Time Series</em>. <a
                            class="framer-text" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07815" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2403.07815</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">A foundation model that treats a numeric series as a
                        sequence of tokens, the way a language model treats text. A candidate in the comparison, with no
                        presumption of superiority.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-34" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Das, A. et al. (2024). <em
                            class="framer-text">A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting</em>. <a
                            class="framer-text" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10688" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2310.10688</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">The other family of pretrained models in the comparison,
                        trained on a large corpus of series to forecast without refitting. Its size makes it the
                        project's CPU-feasibility test.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-35" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Angelopoulos, A.&nbsp;N., Bates, S.,
                        Fisch, A., Lei, L. and Schuster, T. (2022, revised 2025). <em class="framer-text">Conformal Risk
                            Control</em>. <a class="framer-text" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.02814" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2208.02814</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Extends conformal prediction to risk notions broader than
                        coverage rate, with a guarantee that holds on average under exchangeability. That is the
                        assumption the dependence of financial series puts under strain, as the project sheet
                        acknowledges.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-36" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Madaan, A., Tandon, N., Gupta, P.,
                        Hallinan, S., Gao, L., Wiegreffe, S. et al. (2023). <em class="framer-text">Self-Refine:
                            Iterative Refinement with Self-Feedback</em>, NeurIPS. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17651" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2303.17651</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">A loop where one model produces, critiques its own output,
                        then corrects it, with no external signal. It predates the Ralph technique and establishes that
                        critique-and-correct iteration is in no way specific to it.</span></li>
                <li id="ref-37" class="framer-text"><cite class="framer-text">Shinn, N., Cassano, F., Berman, E.,
                        Gopinath, A., Narasimhan, K. and Yao, S. (2023). <em class="framer-text">Reflexion: Language
                            Agents with Verbal Reinforcement Learning</em>, NeurIPS. <a class="framer-text"
                            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366" target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener">arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366</a></cite> <span
                        class="drz-biblio-note framer-text">Keeps a memory of natural-language comments across attempts,
                        standing in for a learning signal. A second antecedent showing that memory between turns also
                        predates the Ralph technique.</span></li>
            </ol>
            <p dir="auto" class="framer-text framer-styles-preset-1gkca5q">Two trails to keep going. <a
                    class="framer-text" href="https://deraison.ai/en/ai-books">My favourite AI
                    books</a>, for the annotated survey. The <a class="framer-text"
                    href="https://sprezzature.ai/figures.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sprezzature gallery</a>,
                to
                see what a body of figures looks like once
                every one of them has been through the loop.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="framer-1akl9mx" data-border="true" data-framer-name="Article Navigation">
            <div class="framer-130n4e5" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="transform:none">
                <p dir="auto" style="--framer-font-size:14px;--framer-line-height:1.4em;--framer-text-color:#555555"
